{
  "LocalisationPackage": {
    "Language": "en-US",
    "Strings": {
      "AIRCRAFT.DESCRIPTION.F35A": "The F-35A Lightning II is a single-seat, conventional takeoff and landing, fifth generation fighter jet built by American aviation firm Lockheed Martin. Dubbed by some a “flying supercomputer,” the stealth, supersonic F-35A boasts a remarkable suite of capabilities, from engaging enemy fighters in air superiority missions, to gathering and processing a tremendous field of intelligence data, to conducting precision bombing and attack raids on even the most heavily defended targets, and much more. The jet entered service on August 2, 2016 with the United States Air Force, its primary user, and is also flown by the militaries of a number of U.S. partner nations, including South Korea, Norway, and Italy, among others.\n\nThe F-35A features a main wing with a span of 35 feet and a swept leading edge, twin canted vertical stabilizers, and all-moving horizontal stabilizers. It is powered by a single Pratt & Whitney F135-PW-100 afterburning turbofan engine that generates up to 28,000 pounds of thrust in military power and up to 43,000 pounds with afterburner. The Lightning II has a range of 1,500 miles, a service ceiling of 50,000 feet above sea level, and a top speed of Mach 1.6.\n\nA truly remarkable aircraft due to its unprecedented combination of systems and capabilities, the Lockheed Martin F-35A Lightning II is a bird of prey and performance. From within the cockpit, aviators can blast into the heights, pull maneuvers up to +9gs, then cruise high or low and scan the world around them in all conditions, day and night.",
      "AIRCRAFT.DESCRIPTION.F35B": "The F-35B Lightning II is a fifth-generation, low-observable, multi-mission tactical aircraft by Lockheed Martin of the United States. The STOVL, or short takeoff and vertical landing, variant of the F-35 family, the F-35B is used primarily by the United States Marine Corps, and also by the militaries of the United Kingdom and Italy.\n\nNot just a groundbreaking aircraft, the F-35B is one of the most remarkable machines in history. It can launch fully loaded after a short run on virtually any type of airstrip, race through the air at speeds faster than Mach 1.5, perform a range of missions, and then land vertically -- all while maintaining the highest degree of stealth possible.\n\nThe F-35B’s main wing has a span of 35 feet and the aircraft measures 51 feet, 2 inches from nose to tail. Its empennage consists of two fully-movable horizontal tail surfaces and dual outwardly-slanted vertical stabilizers. It is powered by a Pratt & Whitney F135-PW-600 afterburning turbofan engine with a thrust vectoring exhaust nozzle and lift fan for vertical flight. The F-35B has a top speed of Mach 1.6 (roughly 1,200 miles per hour), a 1,000 mile range on internal fuel, and a ceiling of 50,000 feet.\n\nThe F-35B has proven itself to be one of the most versatile aircraft in history, not just in the sheer number of systems it has or missions it can perform, but in how it flies. Pilots can roar into the sky in just 550 feet, blast into the stratosphere at supersonic speeds, and loop, roll, and dive in a rush of maneuvers that pull up to 7.0 gs. And then at the end of the flight, aviators can bring it down smoothly on a spot smaller than a tennis court.",
      "AIRCRAFT.DESCRIPTION.F35C": "The F-35C Lightning II is a single-seat, supersonic stealth military aircraft manufactured by U.S.-based Lockheed Martin. The C model is the carrier-based version of the F-35 and features enlarged wings with folding tips, more fuel storage, strengthened landing gear, and larger control surfaces compared to the other two Lightning II variants.\n\nThe F-35C can undertake a wide variety of tasks including fleet defense, precision strike, intelligence gathering, and air superiority, among other missions. Carrier basing allows F-35C squadrons to be placed anywhere in the world to undertake missions exclusively or as part of a larger task force. The F-35C entered service on February 28, 2019 and is operated by the United States Navy and U.S. Marine Corps.\n\nThe F-35C measures 51 feet, 6 inches in length, features a main wing with a span of 43 feet, and has a retractable tricycle landing gear. Its twin vertical stabilizers are angled outward to enhance its low-observable radar profile, and it uses independently movable stabilators in its tail for pitch control. The jet is powered by a single Pratt & Whitney F135-PW-100 afterburning turbofan engine that develops up to 25,000 pounds of thrust in military power and 40,000 pounds with afterburner. It can fly as fast as 1,218 miles per hour, or Mach 1.6, has a service ceiling of 50,000 feet above sea level, and can pull up to 7.5gs.\n\nThe first naval fifth-generation, stealth aircraft, the F-35C Lightning II by Lockheed Martin is a stunning performer. Apart from its incredible number of combat systems, pilots will revere its ability to leap into the skies and execute any series of maneuvers its instructed, with absolute precision."
    }
  }
}
